{
  "title": "Home and rooms",
  "title_target": "ჩემი სახლი — Chemi sakhli",
  "goal": "Describe where you live, name what's in a room, and put adjectives in front of nouns — including when the noun changes its ending.",
  "bekal": [
    "name types of homes and their rooms",
    "put adjectives in front of nouns",
    "say there is / there isn't something somewhere",
    "name floors and where things are",
    "explain what's missing when renting"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "დიალოგი — Viewing a rental apartment",
      "intro": "Ani is looking for a place to live and views an apartment with the owner, Mzia. Notice the adjectives in front of the nouns — and what happens to their endings when the noun takes another form.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "მზია:",
          "text": "[შემობრძანდით|She-mob-rdzan-dit]! [ეს|Es] [ბინა|bi-na] [პატარაა|p'a-t'a-ra-a], [მაგრამ|mag-ram] [ნათელი|na-te-li].",
          "id": "Come in! This apartment is small, but bright.",
          "literal": "come-in! this — apartment — small-is, but — bright."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[ლამაზია|La-ma-zi-a]. [რამდენი|Ram-de-ni] [ოთახია|o-ta-khi-a]?",
          "id": "Nice. How many rooms are there?",
          "literal": "pretty-is. how-many — room-is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "მზია:",
          "text": "[ორი|O-ri] [ოთახი|o-ta-khi]: [საძინებელი|sa-dzi-ne-be-li] [და|da] [მისაღები|mi-sa-ghe-bi]. [სამზარეულო|Sam-za-re-u-lo] [აქ|ak] [არის|a-ris].",
          "id": "Two rooms: a bedroom and a living room. The kitchen is here.",
          "literal": "two — room: sleep-place — and — receive-place. kitchen — here — is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[სამზარეულოში|Sam-za-re-u-losh-i] [მაცივარი|ma-tsi-va-ri] [არის|a-ris]?",
          "id": "Is there a fridge in the kitchen?",
          "literal": "kitchen-in — fridge — is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "მზია:",
          "text": "[კი|K'i], [მაცივარი|ma-tsi-va-ri], [ღუმელი|ghu-me-li] [და|da] [სარეცხი|sa-rets-khi] [მანქანა|man-ka-na] [არის|a-ris].",
          "id": "Yes, there's a fridge, a stove, and a washing machine.",
          "literal": "yes, fridge, stove — and — washing — machine — is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[ცხელი|Tskhe-li] [წყალი|ts'q'a-li] [მუდამ|mu-dam] [არის|a-ris]?",
          "id": "Is there always hot water?",
          "literal": "hot — water — always — is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "მზია:",
          "text": "[დიახ|Di-akh], [მუდამ|mu-dam]. [ინტერნეტიც|In-t'er-ne-t'its] [სწრაფია|sts'ra-pi-a].",
          "id": "Yes, always. The internet is fast, too.",
          "literal": "yes, always. internet-also — fast-is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[რომელ|Ro-mel] [სართულზეა|sar-tul-ze-a]?",
          "id": "Which floor is it on?",
          "literal": "which — floor-on-is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "მზია:",
          "text": "[მეოთხეზე|Me-ot-khe-ze]. [ლიფტი|Lip-t'i] [არ|ar] [არის|a-ris], [სამწუხაროდ|sam-ts'u-kha-rod].",
          "id": "The fourth. Unfortunately there's no elevator.",
          "literal": "fourth-on. elevator — not — is, unfortunately."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[არა|A-ra] [უშავს|u-shavs]. [რამდენი|Ram-de-ni] [ღირს|ghirs] [თვეში|tve-shi]?",
          "id": "That's okay. How much is it a month?",
          "literal": "not — bad-to-it. how-much — costs — month-in?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "მზია:",
          "text": "[ხუთასი|Khu-ta-si] [ლარი|la-ri], [კომუნალურის|k'o-mu-na-lu-ris] [გარეშე|ga-re-she].",
          "id": "Five hundred lari, utilities not included.",
          "literal": "five-hundred — lari, utilities-of — without."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[კარგი|K'ar-gi]. [ხვალამდე|Khva-lam-de] [ვიფიქრებ|vi-pik-reb], [თუ|tu] [შეიძლება|she-idz-le-ba].",
          "id": "All right. I'll think about it until tomorrow, if that's okay.",
          "literal": "good. tomorrow-until — I-will-think, if — possible."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Adjectives go in front of the noun — just like in English.** დიდი სახლი (big house), ცხელი წყალი (hot water), ლამაზი ქალაქი (beautiful city). ⚠️ Flip the order — *სახლი დიდი* — and it sounds like an unfinished sentence.",
        "**An adjective drops its -ი ending as soon as the noun changes its ending.** დიდი სახლი → დიდ სახლ**ში** (in the big house), ცხელი ჩაი → ცხელ ჩა**ის** (the hot tea, as an object). That is the only change you need to remember at A1.",
        "**\"There is\" and \"is\" are the same word: არის.** *სამზარეულოში მაცივარი არის* — there is a fridge in the kitchen. The negative is *არ არის* (there isn't). Georgian has no separate \"there is/there are\" construction.",
        "**Floors use ordinal numbers, formed with მე-…-ე**: მე**ორ**ე (second), მე**სამ**ე (third), მე**ოთხ**ე (fourth), მე**ხუთ**ე (fifth). The first one is irregular: **პირველი**. The same pattern works for dates and any kind of order.",
        "**არა უშავს = \"that's okay\".** Literally \"it's not bad for it\". One of the most common phrases for reassuring someone or accepting how things are.",
        "**კომუნალური = utilities (electricity, water, gas).** You'll see this word in every rental ad in Georgia, usually as *კომუნალურის გარეშე* (not included). ⚠️ In winter, the gas bill for heating can reach half the rent — ask *ზამთარში რამდენია კომუნალური?* before you sign.",
        "**ხვალამდე = until tomorrow.** The ending **-მდე** means \"until/up to\": ხვალ**ამდე**, საღამო**მდე** (until evening), თბილის**ამდე** (as far as Tbilisi)."
      ],
